1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I'm Dwight Wiest, Scoutmaster with Troop 17 at Yakota Air Base, Japan.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,500
This edition of the Scoutmaster podcast is sponsored by backers like me.

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:14,000
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Over this past week, we've been reminded again and again of the tragic events that unfolded in Orlando, Florida,

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
And I think it's worthy of taking just a moment to think about how this affects our Scouts

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
and what we, as Scouters, can do.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
I want to draw your attention to a post that I wrote a couple years ago,

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
called Talking with Scouts about tragic events,

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
And I'll have a link to it in the podcast notes.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
We understand that when tragedy unfolds, our Scouts know about it,

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,000
They'll discuss it,

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,000
They'll wonder what we think,

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
And we can help them by doing three things:

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,000
We can talk about it,

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
We can help them gain some perspective and we can find constructive actions.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
And I repeat the advice of Fred Rogers, who said this:

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
When I was a boy and I would see scary things in the news, my mother would say to me:

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Look for the helpers.

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
You will always find people who are helping

20
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
To this day, especially in times of disaster. I remember my mother's words

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
And I'm always comforted by realizing that there are still so many helpers,

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
so many caring people in the world.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Scouters are good, caring people.

24
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
When tragedy is like this unfold, it's easy to be cynical,

25
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
It's easy to say that the whole world's going to hell in a handbasket,

26
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And it feels that way sometimes.

27
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
But here's my antidote:

28
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,000
I look at my Scouts.

29
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
I see the promise in their lives

30
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And I know that Scouting will contribute to the kind of good people

31
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
they'll become in the future, the helpers they'll become.

32
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
This is podcast number 321..

