and I'll be sure to thank you personally on next week's podcast.

98
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
In this week's podcast I've got something to talk about at Scout Master Ship in seven minutes or less.

99
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
It might go a little over seven minutes,

100
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
We'll find out though, right,

101
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
But I hope you find it useful and let's get started, shall we?

102
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Scout Master Ship in seven minutes or less.

103
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
If you'll take a look at a map of Great Britain,

104
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,000
all the way down in the southeast corner is a place called Pool and Pool Harbor.

105
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:41,000
And one afternoon back in 1907 the captain, or the adult who was in charge of the pool company Boys Brigade,

106
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:47,000
called three members of his brigade aside because he had received a very unusual request.

107
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:53,000
He had been asked to choose three of his boys for a week-long camp

108
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:00,000
nearby Brown Sea Island in Pool Harbor, with nobody less than General Robert Baden Powell.

109
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
So if you think about that for a moment, you know

110
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,000
the Boys Brigade was an organization that existed before Baden Powell started the scouting movement

111
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:16,000
and he was getting ready to test out some of his theories very practically in a camp on Brown Sea Island,

112
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
which if you've been around scouting for a while, you recognize the place name right.

113
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And so these three boys, they're out there doing what Boys Brigade guys do,

114
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
which was like a military drill. they're playing sports and things like that.

115
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:35,000
and the captain of the brigade says: hey, had this invitation. I think you three guys ought to go.

116
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:40,000
The prospect of meeting General Baden Powell must have been extraordinarily exciting,

117
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000
because he was at the time a celebrated national hero-

118
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
He was a superstar in Great Britain at the time-

119
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:55,000
and not just getting to meet the man, but to go on a week-long camp with him out on this island.

120
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
I mean, I know as a boy, if somebody had said to me we're gonna go camping,

121
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:03,000
it's gonna be for a week, we're going out on this island. what could be better, right?

122
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:09,000
So those three boys joined 18 others on Brown Sea in the shelter of Pool Harbor. there,

123
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
for scouting's first practical test camp.

124
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,000
They slept under canvas for the first time in their lives,

125
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,000
they played games and they practiced skills. and imagine sitting around a campfire

126
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:28,000
with General Baden Powell- a national hero, and listen to him tell stories.

127
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:36,000
And what I was thinking about, reflecting back on this, is what a moment that must have been.

128
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Imagine those boys stepping onto the island, seeing the tents off in the distance,

129
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
smelling the campfire smoke, looking into the faces of newfound friends,

130
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,000
shaking the hand of General Robert Baden Powell,

131
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
hearing the rain falling on a tent just above your head.

132
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
I mean, can you get there with me?

133
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Can you feel what that moment must have been like for those boys?

134
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Now? they had no way of knowing they were pioneering a movement

135
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
for millions of young people around the world.

136
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Several of those boys that were on Brown Sea Island in 1907

137
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:13,000
lived well into their old age to vividly recount their experiences

138
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
decades and decades later.

139
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:21,000
That moment- let's call it the Brown Sea Island moment of reverie

140
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
and uncertainty and joy and maybe a little trepidation,

141
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Like when you look off in the distance you see a thunderstorm coming at you

142
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,000
or you're up before the sun gets up

143
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
and the dawn just begins to break over the trees.

144
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Just that moment is something that we get to see as scouters,

145
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
over and over and over again in the lives of our scouts.

146
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
I mean, the pathways of scouting have been around for a long time.

147
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
They're worn by travel, but each new scout encounters

148
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
a new and unexplored land.

149
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,000
The same compelling moment happens for the young people we work with,

150
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
just as it did for those boys over a century ago.

151
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Do you know what I'm talking about? Have you seen it?

152
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Step back and watch.

153
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
Watch for that Brown Sea Island moment.

154
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
You can see it in your scouts.

155
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:18,000
You can see the anticipation, the reverie, the excitement,

156
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
And sometimes you can see them hold back a little bit,

157
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
be a little bit afraid of what they've gotten themselves into.

158
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
And that's all good,

159
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
But watch for that moment and make the best of it.

160
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
It's such a wonderful opportunity,

161
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:35,000
But before I go, let's pay attention to an equally important moment

162
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
that preceded that Brown Sea Island moment.

163
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,000
So think about this:

164
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:47,000
The parents of those first scouts had to stand there at the dock,

165
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
put their boys on the boat, wish them well

166
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and watch that boat, leave the dock and head for the island.

167
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
Now that must have been quite a moment too for those parents.

168
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Can you imagine all the different things that they were thinking?

169
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
They were proud that their sons had been selected,

170
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
or going off with this august personage on a very exciting adventure.

171
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:12,000
They were probably worried to a certain extent about how this would all go

172
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and whether or not the boys would do well.

173
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,000
And you know, I can also imagine maybe being a little resentful

174
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
of that opportunity and saying: you know,

175
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
when I was a kid I never had that chance.

176
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
I mean, most of the people I'm talking to right now are parents,

177
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:28,000
and you know what I'm talking about.

178
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:32,000
There's a moment when you launch your child off into the world

179
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:37,000
in one way or another, where there's a lot of things going on. there aren't there,

180
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,000
And so you have to ask yourself: when does your presence as a parent

181
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,000
kind of cross the line from expressing support and lending stability

182
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:50,000
to being maybe a little intrusive or being a little over-involved,

183
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:55,000
When does rational concern for the well-being of your children

184
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
turn into paranoia?

185
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
And you know, hey, look, cut yourself a break,

186
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Don't worry too much about it.

187
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:03,000
I understand

188
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
Any parent's good intentions can go awry when our culture plays

189
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:12,000
on our natural parental fears with this constant barrage

190
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
of negative news that drives us towards extraordinary measures

191
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
to keep our children physically and psychologically safe.

192
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
I think it's something that every parent worries about,

193
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,000
and probably every parent that ever was worried about to some extent or another.

194
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,000
But we don't want to obsess over those things

195
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:34,000
because we miss the moment that creates the Brown Sea Island moment.

196
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:35,000
Do you see what I'm saying?

197
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
If we're not willing to let go and to trust the opportunity

198
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,000
and the processes that work, that Brown Sea Island moment

199
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
can't really be created, can it?

200
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
It's just something to think about.

201
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:51,000
I mean, our culture encourages us to get involved in our children's lives

202
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,000
and we now have the technology to be in touch with them

203
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
on a moment-to-moment basis, to recognize and curate

204
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
every single moment of their lives.

205
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
And the boundaries get a little blurry, don't they?

206
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
I mean our ability to offer constant unsolicited comment,

207
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
criticism and quote helpful unquote suggestions

208
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
might be a little overwhelming.

209
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
And you know, if we're honest as parents,

210
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
we'll admit that sometimes we try to fulfill

211
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,000
our own longing's needs through our children.

212
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
Our attempt to ring every last drop of significance

213
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
from every moment can be a little selfish,

214
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
And I've experienced that too, and I know how tempting it is

215
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,000
to try and freeze our relationship with our children

216
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,000
as it is now rather than accept that it is going to change.

217
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
But it must change for them to grow,

218
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
It must change for them to get to experience

219
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
that Brown Sea Island moment.

220
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Now, I'm not suggesting that your involvement as a parent

221
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
with your child in Scouts is a negative influence,

222
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:54,000
by no means.

223
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,000
All I'm saying is is that, being aware that there are

224
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
opportunities for that Brown Sea Island moment right,

225
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
That are created by the previous moment

226
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
They're at the dock, when you put them on the boat,

227
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:08,000
You wish them well

228
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
And you watch as they're off to that next adventure.

229
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Your involvement is important,

230
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
but so is the opportunity for your child

231
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
to have that kind of experience.

232
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:21,000
So watch for it,

233
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Watch for that opportunity,

234
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:24,000
Make the best of it,

235
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
And our Scouts will continue to have

236
